WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.153
Go's explicit errors are useful,

2
00:00:02.233 --> 00:00:05.067
but API security depends on how those
errors are handled.

3
00:00:05.147 --> 00:00:09.222
Internal errors may include database
details, downstream service information,

4
00:00:09.302 --> 00:00:12.313
file paths, configuration names, or
implementation clues.

5
00:00:12.393 --> 00:00:16.172
Clients need useful failure information,
but they do not need stack traces,

6
00:00:16.252 --> 00:00:19.617
raw queries, secrets, tokens, cookies, or
private configuration.

7
00:00:19.697 --> 00:00:23.181
A secure API maps internal failures to
safe client-facing responses.

8
00:00:23.261 --> 00:00:26.863
Validation errors, authentication
failures, authorization denials,

9
00:00:26.943 --> 00:00:28.950
missing records, dependency timeouts,

10
00:00:29.030 --> 00:00:33.223
and unexpected internal failures should
have consistent status codes and response

11
00:00:33.303 --> 00:00:33.716
shapes.

12
00:00:33.796 --> 00:00:37.988
The response should help a legitimate
caller recover without exposing details that

13
00:00:38.068 --> 00:00:39.308
belong in internal logs.

14
00:00:39.388 --> 00:00:42.340
Logging and observability should support
investigation.

15
00:00:42.420 --> 00:00:46.317
Structured logs, request IDs, correlation
IDs, route names, caller context,

16
00:00:46.397 --> 00:00:49.822
timing, and outcome fields can help
operators understand behavior.

17
00:00:49.902 --> 00:00:53.209
Security-relevant events may include
authentication outcomes,

18
00:00:53.289 --> 00:00:57.127
authorization denials, sensitive state
changes, administrative actions,

19
00:00:57.207 --> 00:01:00.159
validation spikes, and unusual
resource-control events.

20
00:01:00.239 --> 00:01:03.309
Redaction is essential because logs rarely
stay in one place.

21
00:01:03.389 --> 00:01:07.404
They may flow into dashboards, alerting
systems, tickets, long-term archives,

22
00:01:07.484 --> 00:01:08.665
and third-party tools.

23
00:01:08.745 --> 00:01:12.642
Raw request bodies, API keys, passwords,
tokens, cookies, private user data,

24
00:01:12.722 --> 00:01:15.379
and sensitive records should not be logged
casually.

25
00:01:15.459 --> 00:01:17.762
Good observability is controlled, not
blind.

26
00:01:17.842 --> 00:01:20.204
Log enough to detect and investigate
problems,

27
00:01:20.284 --> 00:01:23.531
but avoid recording data that creates a
second exposure channel.

28
00:01:23.611 --> 00:01:26.741
Document what is logged, what is redacted,
who can access logs,

29
00:01:26.821 --> 00:01:29.478
and how long sensitive operational data is
retained.

30
00:01:29.558 --> 00:01:33.632
Teams should also separate user-facing
error language from internal diagnostic

31
00:01:33.712 --> 00:01:34.125
detail.

32
00:01:34.205 --> 00:01:37.807
A wrapped internal error can help
engineers find the source of a failure,

33
00:01:37.887 --> 00:01:40.898
while the HTTP response remains stable and
safe for clients.

34
00:01:40.978 --> 00:01:45.289
That separation keeps operations useful
without exposing implementation details or

35
00:01:45.369 --> 00:01:47.140
sensitive context unnecessarily.
